Bake the static Firefox theme from the shipped colour schemes

A user without the generator reaches no sheet in a profile, and so
does a user of another operating system. The add-on channel of Firefox
carries a theme to both of them. The colour keys of that channel take
the palette of the sheet.

The manifest mode reads the two shipped colour files and writes one
manifest of two halves. Both halves walk the map of the sheet, so one
row moves the two arms together. A check reads every row of the two
arms against each other. The frame colour stays flat, and a titlebar
appearance that carries a gradient adds one gradient image beside it.
The shipped Alpenglow theme holds that shape.

The version of the add-on is its own number line, and it starts at
1.0. The project version moves under other rules, so a release of
QtCurve moves no version of the add-on. A date stamp would break the
byte identical manifest that the check suite and the package need. The
two deprecated colour aliases never ship, and a check asserts the pair
absent. The loader drops a key that it does not know without a word.

The archive holds the manifest and the card image of about:addons.
firefox/preview/render.py writes that image from the dark half, and
the manifest gains the description and the author of the card. One
source tree gives one manifest, byte for byte, and a check reads that
repeat against the member of the archive. The archive itself takes
other bytes at each build. A zip member carries the times of its file,
and no step of the build sets the change time of an inode. No rule of
this tree writes the add-on directory of a browser, because the place
of such a file is a decision of the distribution.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 5 of 12. The branch holds those twelve commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

